---
// Gizlilik politikası. Gerçek davranışa dayanır: sunucu (server/README.md, server/src/account.ts, cleanup.ts,
// transcribe.ts, deepgram.ts, llm/, auth/, billing.ts, trialLedger.ts, deviceCheck.ts, migrations/, wrangler.jsonc), abonelik uygulaması
// (App/Hosted: canlı metin Deepgram'la, kapasite dolunca ya da bağlantı yoksa Mac'te; iCloud eşitlemesi yok, güncellemeler
// GitHub'dan) ve bu sitenin tarayıcı depolaması (public/theme.js,
// src/client/session.ts). Sağlayıcıların saklama ve eğitim kuralları resmî belgelerinden alınmıştır (2026-10-29):
// ElevenLabs (elevenlabs.io/dpa, docs "Zero Retention Mode" ve "Data residency", terms-of-use-eu §4), Anthropic
// (anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms ve /legal/data-processing-addendum, privacy.claude.com saklama makalesi,
// platform.claude.com "Data residency"), Link (link.com/terms "Sold Through Link Terms", link.com/privacy), Deepgram
// (developers.deepgram.com "Your Data at Deepgram" ve "Model Improvement Partnership Program": `mip_opt_out=true` ile ses
// ve metin yalnızca istek işlenirken tutulur, model geliştirmede kullanılmaz; kullanım kayıtları içerik içermez, 90 gün).
import LegalSection from "../components/LegalSection.astro";
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { cardStatementDescriptor, productName } from "../config";
import { copy } from "../copy";
import type { LegalSectionInfo } from "../lib/legal";
import { operator } from "../legal/operator";
import { routes } from "../routes";

const sections: readonly LegalSectionInfo[] = [
  { id: "controller", title: "Who is responsible" },
  { id: "summary", title: "The short version" },
  { id: "meetings", title: "Your meeting content" },
  { id: "data", title: "Data we process, why, and on what legal basis" },
  { id: "messages", title: "Contact form, email, feedback and crash reports" },
  { id: "website", title: "This website, cookies and browser storage" },
  { id: "providers", title: "Service providers and international transfers" },
  { id: "retention", title: "How long we keep data" },
  { id: "deletion", title: "Deleting your account" },
  { id: "rights", title: "Your rights" },
  { id: "security", title: "Security" },
  { id: "decisions", title: "Automated decisions" },
  { id: "children", title: "Children" },
  { id: "changes", title: "Changes to this policy" },
  { id: "contact", title: "Contact" },
];
const mail: string = `mailto:${operator.email}`;
/** Ödeme tarafının (Stripe, Link) tüketiciye açık metinleri. */
const stripePrivacyUrl: string = "https://stripe.com/privacy";
const linkPrivacyUrl: string = "https://link.com/privacy";
const linkTermsUrl: string = "https://link.com/terms#sold-through-link-terms";
---

<Legal page="privacy" meta={copy.privacyMeta} title={copy.common.nav.privacy} sections={sections}>
  <p class="legal-lead">
    This policy explains what personal data is processed when you use {productName} — the Mac app, the service behind
    it and this website — why it is processed, who it is shared with, how long it is kept and how you can use your
    rights under the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).
  </p>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="controller">
    <p>
      {productName} is operated by <strong>{operator.name}</strong>, {operator.legalForm}, business ID (Y-tunnus) {operator.businessId},{" "}
      {operator.address} (“we”, “us”). We are the controller for the personal data described in this policy, with one
      exception: for the personal data of other people in the meetings you record (their voices, what they say, their
      names), you are the controller and we process that data on your behalf as your processor (see section 3 and the{" "}
      <a href={`${routes.terms}#dpa`}>data processing terms</a>). For any privacy question or request, write to{" "}
      <a href={mail}>{operator.email}</a>.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="summary">
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your notes, transcripts and recordings are stored on your Mac. Our servers keep no audio, notes or translations;
        the only exception is the final transcript, held encrypted for up to 2 hours so it can be re-sent if the
        connection drops, then deleted.
      </li>
      <li>
        Meeting audio and text go only to our AI providers to be transcribed and summarised: Deepgram for the live
        transcript (directly from your Mac), ElevenLabs for the final transcript and Anthropic for notes (both through our
        server, which keeps no audio or notes and holds the final transcript encrypted for up to 2 hours). Deepgram keeps no
        audio or text after transcribing; the others may keep request data for
        a time under their own retention rules (see section 7). We keep only usage numbers (how long, how many tokens) to
        apply the plan limits.
      </li>
      <li>
        We set no cookies of our own and use no advertising trackers on this website. We count visits with Cloudflare Web
        Analytics, which uses no cookies and collects no personal data. Apple and Google sign-in and Cloudflare Turnstile
        may use their own cookies on the pages where you use them (see section 6). We don't sell your data.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can delete your account yourself at any time; the data on our server is deleted immediately, except a
        pseudonymous record of how many free-trial meetings your sign-in identity has used (see section 9).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="meetings">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Live transcript.</strong> While you record, the app streams the meeting audio directly from your Mac to
        Deepgram, which returns the live transcript. Our server only issues a short-lived access key for each connection
        and records how long the live transcript may run; it never receives this audio. The app asks Deepgram not to keep
        the audio or transcript and not to use them to improve its models. When the capacity we set aside for this is used
        up, when Deepgram can't be reached, or when you're offline, the live transcript is produced on your Mac by an
        on-device speech model instead, and that audio does not leave your Mac.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Final transcript and speaker separation.</strong> When a meeting ends, the app sends the recording
        through our server to ElevenLabs, which returns the transcript. Our server streams the audio through without
        writing it to storage and records its duration. It keeps the returned transcript encrypted for up to 2 hours, so
        the app can fetch it again if the reply is lost on the way; after that it is deleted automatically, or immediately
        when you delete your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notes, live translation, “What did I miss?” and Ask.</strong> The relevant parts of the transcript (and
        of earlier notes, when you use Ask), together with the participant names and terms you have added, are sent
        through our server to Anthropic's Claude model, and the answer returns to your Mac. Our server does not store or
        log this text; it records only the number of tokens used.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storage.</strong> Notes, transcripts, recordings and the people and terms you add are stored in the app's
        folder on your Mac. The subscription app does not sync them to any cloud service. You can export or delete them in
        the app at any time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Integrations.</strong> When you send a note or tasks to Notion, Slack, Microsoft Teams, Discord, Zoom,
        Linear, Jira, Reminders or Apple Notes, save it as Markdown to a folder you choose (for example an Obsidian
        vault), or send it to a webhook you set up (such as Zapier, Make or n8n), the app sends that content from your Mac
        directly to the destination you chose, using the account or address you connected. It does not pass through our
        server, and the receiving service processes it under its own terms. If you connect an AI assistant on your Mac
        through the app's MCP connection, that assistant can read your meetings and handles them under its own terms.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Whose data, and who decides.</strong> For your own voice, your words and the content you add, we process
      meeting content to perform our contract with you. Meetings also involve other people. You decide whether to record a
      meeting and what to do with the result, so for the other participants' personal data you are the controller and we
      act as your processor under GDPR Article 28: we process it only on your instructions — to transcribe the meeting and
      write your note — using the sub-processors listed in section 7, and never for any other purpose. You are responsible
      for telling participants that you are recording and, where the law requires it, getting their consent.
      Participants who want to use their rights should contact the person who recorded the meeting; if they contact us,
      we will pass the request on or help that person answer it.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="data">
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="stack-table table-data">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Legal basis (GDPR Art. 6(1))</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Meeting content.</strong> Meeting audio, transcripts, notes, the questions you ask, and the
              participant names and terms you add. Passed through our server only while it is processed; no audio or notes
              are stored on our servers, and the final transcript is held encrypted for up to 2 hours so it can be
              re-sent.
            </td>
            <td>Transcribing meetings; writing notes, translations and answers</td>
            <td>
              (b) contract, for your own data. For other participants' data, you are the controller and we process it as
              your processor (section 3).
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Account.</strong> The account identifier Apple or Google gives us when you sign in. Their sign-in
              token may also contain your email address; we don't store it.
            </td>
            <td>Creating your account and signing you in</td>
            <td>(b) contract</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Sessions.</strong> One-way hashes of sign-in tokens, with creation and expiry times</td>
            <td>Keeping you signed in; detecting stolen tokens</td>
            <td>(b) contract; (f) legitimate interest in security</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Device attestation.</strong> Where your Mac supports it: Apple App Attest key identifier, public key and
              attestation receipt, app version
            </td>
            <td>Checking that requests come from our genuine app; preventing misuse of the free trial</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in preventing abuse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Usage.</strong> Start and end time and length of each meeting session, audio duration and AI tokens
              used; daily and monthly totals. For each meeting session we also note when its first note was finished and,
              if a request for it failed, how many failed and the last error code (for example “transcription service
              unavailable”) — no content
            </td>
            <td>Applying the trial and plan limits; controlling costs; finding and fixing problems</td>
            <td>(b) contract</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Free-trial abuse prevention.</strong> (a) Apple DeviceCheck: Apple lets us store two bits for each
              device with Apple, and we use one of them to record that a Mac has already been used for a free trial. The app
              sends a short-lived token from Apple; we never receive a device identifier, and the bits are stored by Apple,
              not by us. (b) A pseudonymous keyed one-way hash of your sign-in identity (Apple or Google account
              identifier), stored together with the number of free-trial meetings used, when your trial started,
              whether a Mac was verified for your trial and the current calendar month's usage totals (recorded time and
              AI token counts, no content). We keep it after you delete your account, so deleting and re-creating an
              account does not give a new free trial or reset that month's usage limits.
            </td>
            <td>Preventing the same person or Mac from getting the free trial again (fraud prevention)</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in preventing fraud and abuse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Network data.</strong> Your IP address, briefly, for rate limiting; and a keyed one-way hash of your IP
              address with the date when a new account starts its first trial meeting
            </td>
            <td>
              Preventing abuse and bulk account creation (we may refuse new free trials when many accounts start from the
              same network on the same day)
            </td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in preventing abuse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Subscription.</strong> Stripe customer and subscription identifiers, subscription status, period
              end. Card and payment details are entered at Stripe and never reach us.
            </td>
            <td>Managing your subscription and access</td>
            <td>(b) contract; (c) legal obligation for accounting records</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Cancellation reason.</strong> If you cancel and choose a reason or write a comment in Stripe's
              cancellation form, Stripe passes it to us: the reason you picked (for example “too expensive”), your comment
              and Stripe's own reason (for example a failed payment). It is linked only to your account ID.
            </td>
            <td>Understanding why people cancel and improving the product</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in improving the service</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Technical logs.</strong> Endpoint, result or error code, account ID and time. Logs never contain
              meeting audio or text. We also keep daily error totals (for example how many requests were refused because a
              limit was reached), not linked to anyone.
            </td>
            <td>Operating the service, finding errors, security</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in a reliable and secure service</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Messages and reports.</strong> See section 5.</td>
            <td>Answering you; fixing problems</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest; (b) contract for support requests</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Downloads and updates.</strong> The app and its updates are downloaded from GitHub, which processes
              your IP address and browser or app information to deliver the files (see section 7). We count downloads per
              day, by which button on our site was used and by the domain of the referring page (only the domain, never the
              full address); no IP address or other identifier is stored with the counts. To count each network once a day,
              we keep a keyed one-way hash of your IP address with the date until the end of that day.
            </td>
            <td>Delivering the app and its updates; knowing how many people download it</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in distributing the app</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Website statistics.</strong> Aggregated page views, referring sites, countries and device types,
              counted by Cloudflare Web Analytics without cookies or personal data (see section 6)
            </td>
            <td>Understanding how the website is used and improving it</td>
            <td>(f) legitimate interest in a useful website</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Where we rely on legitimate interest, we have weighed it against your interests and kept the data to the minimum
      needed. You can object at any time (see section 10).
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="messages">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Contact form.</strong> When you write to us through the <a href={routes.contact}>contact form</a>, we
        store your email address and message so that we can answer you. The form is protected by Cloudflare Turnstile
        (see section 6). We delete the message and email address 180 days after we receive them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Email.</strong> If you email us directly, or we answer you by email, the messages are kept in our mailbox,
        hosted by Zoner in Finland, for as long as needed to handle your request and any follow-up.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Feedback and crash reports from the app.</strong> These are sent only when you choose to send them. They
        contain your message or the crash report, the app version and the macOS version, and — if you are signed in —
        your account ID; an email address is included only if you add one. We use them to answer you and to fix problems,
        and delete them 180 days after we receive them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="website">
    <p>
      This website sets no cookies of its own and uses no advertising or tracking tools. Fonts are served from our own
      domain. The site stores the following in your browser:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Theme preference</strong> (<code>localStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.theme</code>): saved only if you
        use the light/dark switch, so the site remembers your choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign-in session</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.session</code>): your session
        tokens after you sign in, kept only in that browser tab and removed when you close the tab or sign out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>App sign-in check</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.appSignIn</code>): a random
        check value while you sign in to the Mac app from the browser, so the answer can only go back to that app; removed
        when sign-in finishes or the tab closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google sign-in check</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.siteSignIn</code>): random
        check values while you sign in on this site with Google, so only your own sign-in is accepted when Google sends
        you back; removed as soon as you're back or the tab closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tab identifier</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.tab</code>): a random value that
        lets a duplicated tab notice it is a copy, so it asks you to sign in again instead of signing you out everywhere.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are strictly necessary for features you ask for, so they don't require consent. Third-party services load
      only on the pages that need them. On the <a href={routes.signIn}>sign-in page</a>, Apple's sign-in script is loaded
      from Apple, and signing in with Google takes you to Google's own sign-in page; Apple and Google may use their own
      cookies and process your IP address and browser information when you sign in, under their privacy policies. On the{" "}
      <a href={routes.contact}>contact page</a>, Cloudflare Turnstile checks that the form is used by a person; it
      processes technical data such as your IP address and browser characteristics. Cloudflare, which hosts the site,
      processes IP addresses to deliver and protect it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Visitor statistics.</strong> To see how the site is used, we use Cloudflare Web Analytics, with Cloudflare as
      our processor. Its script sets no cookies, uses no local or session storage and doesn't fingerprint your browser or
      follow you across sites. We see only aggregated numbers: page views, referring sites, countries and device types.
      Cloudflare doesn't use your IP address or browser details to identify you. Our legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding and improving the website (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="providers">
    <p>
      We use the following providers. Processors handle personal data only on our instructions, under a data processing
      agreement with us; independent controllers decide themselves how they use the data, under their own privacy
      policies.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="stack-table table-processors">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Provider</th><th scope="col">Role and purpose</th><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Location, safeguards and retention</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Cloudflare, Inc.</td>
            <td>Processor: hosting this website and our server (Workers), database (D1), rate limiting, Turnstile, Web Analytics, logs</td>
            <td>Account, usage, message and technical data; IP addresses; meeting content in transit</td>
            <td>
              Database stored in the EU. Cloudflare's network is global and the company is based in the USA: EU–US Data
              Privacy Framework and Standard Contractual Clauses.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ElevenLabs (Eleven Labs Inc.)</td>
            <td>Processor (sub-processor for meeting content): transcribing recordings and separating speakers</td>
            <td>Meeting audio and the resulting transcript</td>
            <td>
              USA: we use ElevenLabs' standard API, which stores customer data in the USA. EU–US Data Privacy Framework and
              Standard Contractual Clauses under its data processing addendum. ElevenLabs offers zero-retention processing
              only to its enterprise customers, so it may keep the audio and transcripts it processes for us under its own
              retention rules; data it deletes stays in its backups for up to 30 days. Its terms allow it to use customer
              content to improve its models unless the customer opts out; we have opted out.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Deepgram, Inc.</td>
            <td>Processor (sub-processor for meeting content): producing the live transcript while you record</td>
            <td>Meeting audio streamed from your Mac, the resulting live transcript, and the participant names and terms you add</td>
            <td>
              USA. Standard Contractual Clauses under Deepgram's data processing agreement. The app opts every connection out
              of Deepgram's Model Improvement Program, so Deepgram keeps the audio and transcript only for as long as it takes
              to process the request and does not use them to train its models. Deepgram keeps request metadata and usage
              logs (no audio, text or transcripts); they are available to us for 90 days.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Anthropic (Anthropic Ireland, Limited, and Anthropic, PBC)</td>
            <td>Processor (sub-processor for meeting content): writing notes, translations and answers with the Claude model</td>
            <td>The relevant parts of meeting transcripts and notes, your questions, and the participant names and terms you add</td>
            <td>
              Stored in the USA; the model may run in other countries where Anthropic operates. Standard Contractual
              Clauses under Anthropic's data processing addendum. Anthropic does not train its models on this data and
              deletes it within 30 days; if a request is flagged under its usage policy, it may keep it for up to 2 years.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stripe and Link</td>
            <td>Independent controllers: checkout, payment, tax, receipts and refunds; Link is the merchant of record</td>
            <td>Email address, billing address, payment method, purchase and tax details</td>
            <td>
              Ireland and the USA, with the safeguards described in the <a href={stripePrivacyUrl}>Stripe privacy policy</a>{" "}
              and <a href={linkPrivacyUrl}>Link's privacy policy</a>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Apple</td>
            <td>Independent controller: Sign in with Apple; App Attest and DeviceCheck for our request and free-trial checks</td>
            <td>Sign-in identifier and email address (if you share it); device checks, without a device identifier reaching us</td>
            <td>Ireland and the USA, under Apple's privacy policy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google</td>
            <td>Independent controller: Sign in with Google</td>
            <td>Google account identifier and email address</td>
            <td>Ireland and the USA, under Google's privacy policy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>GitHub, Inc.</td>
            <td>Hosting the app's downloads and updates</td>
            <td>IP address and browser or app information when you download the app or it checks for updates</td>
            <td>USA; EU–US Data Privacy Framework, under GitHub's privacy statement</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Zoner Oy</td>
            <td>Processor: hosting our email</td>
            <td>Emails you send us and our replies</td>
            <td>Finland (EU)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>International transfers.</strong> When personal data is transferred outside the EU and EEA, we rely on the
      European Commission's adequacy decision for the EU–US Data Privacy Framework where the recipient is certified under
      it, and otherwise on the Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses included in the provider's data processing terms.
      You can ask us for a copy of the relevant safeguards.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Payments.</strong> Subscriptions are sold through Link, Stripe's payment service, which is the merchant of
      record for your purchase; your card statement shows <code>{cardStatementDescriptor}</code>. Stripe and Link process
      your payment, billing and tax details as independent controllers under the{" "}
      <a href={stripePrivacyUrl}>Stripe privacy policy</a> and <a href={linkPrivacyUrl}>Link's privacy policy</a>, and the
      purchase itself is governed by <a href={linkTermsUrl}>Link's terms</a>. From them we receive only your Stripe customer
      ID, your subscription status and period and, if you cancel and give one, your cancellation reason — never your card
      details. We do not sell personal
      data or share it for advertising.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="retention">
    <ul>
      <li>
        Meeting content: no audio, notes or translations are stored on our servers; the final transcript is kept
        encrypted for up to 2 hours (see section 3), then deleted. At our AI providers: Deepgram keeps the live audio and
        transcript only
        while processing them; Anthropic deletes it within 30 days (up to 2 years if flagged under its usage policy);
        ElevenLabs keeps it under its own retention rules (see section 7).
      </li>
      <li>Account, sign-in identities and device registrations: while your account exists; deleted immediately when you delete it.</li>
      <li>Meeting session records and monthly usage: up to 13 months, then deleted automatically; deleted immediately when you delete your account.</li>
      <li>
        The pseudonymous free-trial record (a keyed hash of your sign-in identity, the number of free-trial meetings used,
        when the trial started and that month's usage totals): for up to 24 months after you delete your account, then
        deleted automatically; the usage totals only matter until the end of that month.
      </li>
      <li>The DeviceCheck bit for your Mac is stored by Apple, not by us; it holds no personal details, only that the Mac has already given a free trial.</li>
      <li>Sign-in tokens: up to 60 days; deleted within a day after they expire or you sign out.</li>
      <li>Daily usage totals: 2 days. The hashed IP records for new trials and for counting downloads: until the end of that day.</li>
      <li>Contact messages, feedback and crash reports: 180 days. Emails: for as long as needed to handle your request.</li>
      <li>Cancellation reasons and comments: 180 days; deleted immediately when you delete your account.</li>
      <li>Processed payment events and total daily AI spend (not linked to you): 90 days.</li>
      <li>Daily download and error totals (not linked to you): 400 days.</li>
      <li>Technical logs: kept for a short period by Cloudflare, normally no more than a few days.</li>
      <li>Database backups (Cloudflare D1 point-in-time recovery): up to 30 days, after which deleted data is gone from backups too.</li>
      <li>
        Our accounting records of subscription income: for as long as Finnish accounting law requires (currently up to 10
        years). Receipts and payment records are kept by Stripe and Link under their own policies.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="deletion">
    <p>
      You can delete your account at any time with “Delete account” on your <a href={routes.account}>account page</a>.
      Any unfinished subscription is first cancelled immediately at Stripe; then your account, sign-in identities,
      sessions, device registrations, meeting session records, usage, subscription record, cancellation reasons and Stripe
      customer link are deleted together in a single step.
    </p>
    <p>
      What remains: a pseudonymous record of your free-trial use (a keyed one-way hash of your sign-in identity, the
      number of free-trial meetings used, when the trial started, whether a Mac was verified and that month's usage
      totals), kept for up to 24 months to prevent abuse — if you sign in again with the same Apple or Google account, your
      new account continues the trial where the old one left off and, within the same month, starts from the old
      account's usage; the DeviceCheck bit for your Mac, which Apple stores; today's trial and download counters for
      your network (a hashed IP address, not linked to any account, deleted at the end of the day); payment records kept by
      Stripe and Link; messages you sent through the contact form until their 180 days run out (ask us if you want them
      deleted sooner — feedback and crash reports linked to your account are deleted with it); if a duplicate
      subscription was ever created by mistake, its Stripe identifiers for up to 400 days so a double charge can be
      refunded; and
      technical logs and backups until they expire. Notes and recordings on your Mac stay where they are; you delete them
      in the app.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="rights">
    <p>Under the GDPR you have the right to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>access the personal data we hold about you and get a copy of it;</li>
      <li>have inaccurate data corrected;</li>
      <li>have your data erased;</li>
      <li>restrict processing;</li>
      <li>receive the data you gave us in a portable format;</li>
      <li>object to processing based on legitimate interest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Write to <a href={mail}>{operator.email}</a>. We may ask you to confirm that the account is yours, and we reply
      within one month. If you took part in a meeting that someone else recorded, contact that person first (see section
      3). You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority — in Finland, the Office of the Data
      Protection Ombudsman (Tietosuojavaltuutetun toimisto, <a href="https://tietosuoja.fi/en">tietosuoja.fi</a>), or the
      authority in the EU country where you live or work.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="security">
    <p>
      All connections are encrypted with TLS. Sign-in tokens are stored on our server only as one-way hashes and IP
      addresses only as keyed hashes. Free trials are checked with Apple DeviceCheck, and requests from the app with Apple
      App Attest where the Mac supports it. Our provider keys exist only
      as encrypted secrets on our server, and access to the service's systems is limited to the operator.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="decisions">
    <p>
      We don't make decisions about you based solely on automated processing that have legal or similarly significant
      effects. Usage limits are applied automatically according to your plan, as described in the{" "}
      <a href={routes.terms}>Terms of service</a>.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="children">
    <p>{productName} is not intended for anyone under 18, and we don't knowingly process children's data.</p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="changes">
    <p>
      When we change this policy we update the date at the top. We announce material changes in the app and on this
      page before they take effect.
    </p>
